  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Saturday...\n\nIsolated showers and a few rumbles of thunder are ongoing this\nmorning, mainly over southeastern Montana. The only concern with\nthis activity are brief wind gusts and a few lightning strikes.\nOtherwise, patchy fog may develop in the lower valleys north and\neast of Billings this morning, including locations near the\nDakotas Border.\n\nAn upper low continues to dig through the Pacific Northwest\ntoday. Later this afternoon and evening, as this low moves\neastward the left exit region of the trough could bring isolated\nto scattered shower and thunderstorm development to south and \ncentral Montana. The Storm Prediction Center has a Marginal Risk \n(level 1 out of 5) across much of Montana, for a few strong to \nsevere thunderstorms. The primary concerns are strong wind gusts, \nheavy rainfall, and hail. Capping looks to be a problem though, \nwith HiRes models depicting a strong enough cap that hinders \nconvective development. The exception is far southeast Montana \nwhere a surface low will exist and provide a forcing mechanism. \n\nThe low continues moving through Idaho this evening, \nbringing a shortwave ahead of it. This wave will bring ample\nforcing and a better chance of precipitation (up to 70 percent) \nto south- central Montana into Monday. Look for showers and weak \nthunderstorms overnight, before the chance for precipitation\ngenerally comes to an end through Monday morning. Lingering\nactivity may still exist over the mountains and foothills west of\nBillings as well as along the Dakotas Border.\n\nThe aforementioned low pressure system will move northeastward \nthrough Montana on Monday. Isolated shower and thunderstorm\nactivity looks to accompany this during the day. The threat for \nstrong to severe thunderstorms will be very low. The main hazard \nlooks to be the synoptic winds associated with the low pressure \nsystem. At this time, widespread wind gusts in the 20s to 40s mph \nare forecast, strongest over the foothills west of Billings. Areas\naround Livingston to Big Timber and Harlowton may even see a few \n50 mph wind gusts late Monday (50 to 70 percent chance). \n\nTuesday, windy conditions look to continue across much of the \narea, though the strongest winds will shift to be over far eastern\nMontana. Again, wind gusts in the 20s to 40s mph are forecast to \nbe common, strongest to the north and east, with a few 50 mph wind\ngusts possible around Baker, Montana (25 to 40 percent chance).\n\nWednesday into Friday are forecast to be mostly dry days as low \namplitude ridging nudges into the region aloft. By late Friday \ninto the weekend, the chance of precipitation returns, but \nuncertainty remains regarding the upper level weather pattern.\n\nLA/PM",
